// THE @filter IS NOT A REFINEMENT, IT IS WHAT KEEPS THIS AUTOMATION FROM
// DESTROYING DATA.
//
// `graph.node.created` fires on EVERY successful write, not only the first:
// executeWrite publishes it unconditionally because MemQL is append-only and
// an update materialises a new row (component/memql/executor_mutation.go --
// the update() path additionally publishes .updated, which is why that event
// exists at all). So without a filter this automation would re-fire on
// setLibraryFileStatus, appendLibraryFileTrainedDomain and archiveLibraryFile.
//
// Re-firing is not harmless. createArtifact's body is a bare `insert{}`, so a
// re-version carries ONLY the fields named here -- and `labels` is not one of
// them, because labels live on the ARTIFACT and the file row has none to
// forward. Every status transition would therefore silently wipe whatever the
// owner had labelled the artifact: the memql#4288 hazard, reached from the
// promotion path instead of the edit path.
//
// `status == "stored"` is the creation state and only the creation state --
// createLibraryFile STAMPS it (it is not a caller argument), and every later
// write moves it on. Keeping the index in step with what analysis learns is
// therefore the analysis pass's job, which is Go and can carry the labels
// forward the way integrations/library's touchArtifact does.
/// On file creation, promote the new v1:library:file into the Library as an artifact index row
/// (lens=artifact, kind=file). The sixth promotion, and the shape of the five above: sourceConceptRef
/// binds event.payload.id directly, the optional fields coalesce so no nil reaches a string field,
/// and the derived id makes a re-run version the same row rather than add a second one. format is
/// carried from the file row, where the writer that knew the MIME wrote it -- an automation step
/// argument has no conditional form, so the mapping lives in Go and its answer lives on the row.
/// memql#4340.
@trigger(event="node.created", concept="v1:library:file", partition="*")
@filter(payload.status == "stored")
automation indexFileOnCreate {
  args {
    archived any
    format any
    id any
    mimeType any
    name any
    ownerUserId any
    source any
    summary any
  }

  step persist {
    mutation createArtifact (
      sourceConceptRef: id,
      ownerUserId:      ownerUserId,
      lens:             "artifact",
      kind:             "file",
      source,
      title:            name ?? "Untitled file",
      summary:          summary ?? "",
      format:           format ?? "other",
      mimeType:         mimeType ?? "",
      live:             false,
      archived:         archived ?? false
    )
  }
}

/// When an artifact backed by a file is archived, archive the backing v1:library:file too, so the
/// two rows never disagree about whether the owner threw the thing away. Triggered on the artifact
/// UPDATE rather than written into archiveArtifact's body because a mutation body writes one row
/// and which second row to touch depends on the kind -- the @filter is where that condition
/// belongs. sourceConceptRef is the canonical 'v1:library:file:<id>' the promotion wrote, which is
/// the id archiveLibraryFile targets. ONE DIRECTION ONLY, deliberately: artifact -> file. The
/// mirror automation (file -> artifact) would close a cycle, since each write publishes an event
/// the other subscribes to, and both writes being idempotent does not stop the events. Archiving
/// through the artifact is the portal's path; archiveLibraryFile is the file-level operation.
/// Idempotent: archiving an already-archived file writes the same value. memql#4340.
@trigger(event="node.updated", concept="v1:library:artifact", partition="*")
@filter(payload.kind == "file" && payload.archived == true)
automation archiveFileOnArtifactArchive {
  args {
    sourceConceptRef any
  }

  step archiveBackingFile {
    mutation archiveLibraryFile (
      fileId: sourceConceptRef
    )
  }
}
